[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mocking Network Requests and Waiting for Aliases in Cypress"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When testing modern web applications, your frontend often communicates with backend APIs. Relying on a live backend during end-to-end testing can lead to flaky tests, slow execution, and unpredictable data states. Cypress provides powerful network stubbing and aliasing capabilities to intercept, mock, and wait for network requests."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding cy.intercept()"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The cy.intercept() command is used to route and stub requests to external URLs or API endpoints. By using cy.intercept(), you can inspect, modify, or completely mock the responses of HTTP requests before they reach your application."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"cy.intercept('GET', '/api/users', { fixture: 'users.json' }).as('getUsers');"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating and Using Aliases"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The .as() command assigns an alias to your intercepted route. This alias can later be referenced using the cy.wait() command. Aliasing allows your test to explicitly pause and synchronize with asynchronous network activity, preventing race conditions."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"cy.intercept('POST', '/api/login').as('loginRequest');
cy.get('#login-btn').click();
cy.wait('@loginRequest').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Step-by-Step Implementation Guide"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 1: Define your network stub using cy.intercept() before triggering the action in your test."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 2: Assign a descriptive alias using the .as() method so it is easily identifiable."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 3: Perform the user action in the UI, such as clicking a submit button or visiting a page."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 4: Use cy.wait('@aliasName') to ensure the request has completed before asserting on the updated DOM."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Complete Code Example"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"describe('User Dashboard Test', () => {
  it('loads user profile successfully', () => {
    cy.intercept('GET', '/api/profile', {
      statusCode: 200,
      body: { id: 1, name: 'Jane Doe', role: 'Admin' }
    }).as('getProfile');

    cy.visit('/dashboard');

    cy.wait('@getProfile');

    cy.get('.profile-name').should('contain', 'Jane Doe');
  });
});"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always set up cy.intercept() before your cy.visit() or user actions to avoid race conditions where the request fires before the interceptor is ready."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use fixtures for large mock payloads instead of hardcoding JSON objects directly inside your test files."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inspect the response object returned by cy.wait() to validate status codes, headers, and payload data."}]
